  "undisclosed_income": {
    "found": true,
    "amount": 50400,
    "source": "HR letter (Granite Peak, 2026-03-02): 2026 target bonus 45,000, paid 50% Sept / 50% Feb, 'currently at 112% of plan' -> expected 2026 bonus 45,000 x 1.12 = 50,400 (conservative floor: 45,000 target). Corroborated by 2026-09-15 pay stub: YTD gross 146,212 vs 119,000 prorated salary = 27,212 unexplained, approx. the 50% September installment (25,200 at 112% of plan). ~2,012 of the stub difference is not explained by the letter - left open. Client further reports (2026-10 turn) Husband always receives a bonus, 2025 approx 40,000 - client recollection only, not employer documentation, so not counted in the 50,400; subpoena 2023-2025 W-2s/plan statements. The sworn affidavit (2026-08-30, after the HR letter) states gross income 168,000 salary only and bonus 'discretionary; none expected for 2026' - contradicted by the employer document; Columbia Rule 16.2(e) requires sworn disclosure of bonuses/expected compensation and allows reopening and sanctions, including an unequal division (columbia_equitable_distribution.md). Guidelines include employer-documented bonuses in gross income (columbia_child_support_guidelines.md)."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "name": "A. Keep proposal's allocations but equalize AFTER TAX: Wife keeps house + 50k cash + Volvo; QDRO transfer of 32,300 from husband's 401(k) to Wife (0.76 x 32,300 = 24,548 post-tax) closes the 48,992 gap. Cheapest fix; Wife still ends up asset-poor in liquid terms and bears the whole house-sell tax burden alone (one 250k exclusion).",
      "wife_after_tax": 637556,
      "husband_after_tax": 637452,
      "instruments": ["QDRO (IRC 414(p)) for 32,300", "quitclaim deed + refinance or hold-harmless on joint mortgage", "equalization clause referencing post-tax values"]
    },
    {
      "name": "B. Sell house NOW while both exclusions are available: sale on joint listing; if still married at year-end and both agree, joint 500,000 IRC 121(b)(1) exclusion shelters gain (tax drops from 90,992 to 43,992, saving 47,000); split everything exactly half post-tax: each receives half of 590,008 net sale proceeds + half brokerage (in-kind, 1041 carryover) + 50k cash + a car, and either half the 401(k) by QDRO (200,000 face) or offsetting cash. Requires husband's cooperation on a joint return - not verified that he will agree.",
      "wife_after_tax": 661004,
      "husband_after_tax": 661004,
      "instruments": ["listing/sale agreement with net-proceeds formula", "QDRO (200,000)", "in-kind brokerage split as IRC 1041 transfer", "agreement to file jointly for the sale year (assumption; cooperation needed)"]
    },
    {
      "name": "C. Client's goal, tax-smart version (PRIMARY RECOMMENDATION): Wife stays in house through middle school (3-6 yrs, client-confirmed objective); husband kept ON TITLE and ON THE EXISTING JOINT LOAN - refinance is NOT required and, per underwriting math, Wife CANNOT qualify for a $400k refinance on $72k salary even counting support (housing ratio 35-59%); Wife pays all carrying costs + hold-harmless/indemnity + husband secured by deed of trust; decree grants Wife exclusive use, so husband is 'treated as using' the residence under IRC 121(d)(3)(A) and his 2-of-5 ownership is met, preserving HIS 250,000 exclusion for the later sale - two 250k exclusions vs one if she buys him out and sells alone. On the agreed trigger date (youngest completes 6th grade, or Wife vacates), sell and split net after 6% costs and each party's own gain tax; equalize NOW with the liquid assets: Wife also takes half brokerage, 50k cash, Volvo, and QDRO of 200,000; each side approx 661,000 after tax (at today's values; price held flat - assumption). Numbers equal Alternative B without forcing the kids to move. If husband refuses to remain on loan/title, sale (Alternative B) is genuinely forced - table this with the Rule 16.2 disclosure leverage.",
      "wife_after_tax": 661004,
      "husband_after_tax": 661004,
      "instruments": ["deferred-sale / co-tenancy agreement (trigger date, cost allocation, sale mechanics, net-proceeds formula)", "deed leaving both on title + deed of trust securing husband's interest", "hold-harmless + joint-loan rider (refinance to Wife's name later is an option, never a gate)", "QDRO (200,000)", "in-kind brokerage split (IRC 1041)", "Form 8332 clause - do NOT release by default"]
    }
  ],
  "maintenance_tax_treatment": "The proposal's language ('deductible to Husband and taxable to Wife') is LEGALLY OBSOLETE and should be struck. For divorce or separation instruments executed after 12/31/2018, alimony is NEITHER deductible by the payor NOR includible by the recipient (TCJA sec. 11051, P.L. 115-97, per irc_1041_121_71.md). Any decree signed in 2026 falls under this rule. Practical effect: Wife keeps all 2,000/month tax-free (72,000 over 36 months at full value) - good - but Husband's claimed tax 'cost/benefit' is wrong; value the stream at face. Child support is never deductible or includible. Note the guideline interaction: maintenance paid is still added to Wife's and subtracted from Husband's income BEFORE computing child support (columbia_child_support_guidelines.md), so each 1,000/month of maintenance moves about 126/month of child support - see child_support.json.",
  "dependents_and_filing_status": "Children (9 and 12) are with Wife 255 nights/yr = 69.9% (client-confirmed), so she is the custodial parent. IRC 152(e): she has the right to claim both children EVERY year unless SHE voluntarily releases a year on Form 8332; the 'alternating years' proposal is a giveaway of her tax attributes and should be rejected unless paid for. A Form 8332 release moves only the child tax credit for the released year - head-of-household status, EITC and the dependent-care credit stay with her regardless (filing_status_dependents.md; dollar amounts of the credits are not in the library - unverified, not quantified). Head of household: she qualifies if a qualifying child lives with her more than half the year and she pays more than half the cost of the home (likely post-decree once she has the house + support income; verify actual cost outlays). Filing status is fixed on Dec 31: a decree final on or before 2026-12-31 means she files single/HoH for 2026 - timing the final before year-end is worth money vs married filing (joint-vs-separate consequences for the house sale year should be planned - see Alternatives B/C)."
}

